A||||13||Coop88||88 Steering Rack||||1||
Z||000000||Coop88||07-16-2001||05:10 PM||||After I discovered I could move my front wheels at 3 and 9 o'clock with the steering wheel locked, I thought I'd replace the inner tie rods. After I removed the rack boots, I put the wheels back on to wiggle. I found that the rack itself(part that attaches to tie rods) moves up and down. It moves more on the driver's side. Is this movement normal? Would a new rack bushing help?<p>[This message has been edited by Coop88 (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||07-16-2001||05:15 PM||||If the rack itself moves up and down, then the rack mounting is bad. I don't know if the 88 is the same, but my 85 has two rubber bushings where the rack mounts to the frame. It sounds like these are going bad. If the rack doesn't move, but the inner tie rod does, then it would be your bushings.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||07-16-2001||05:26 PM||||Are you talking about just the rack (the part the tie rods attach to) moving inside the housing or the whole rack assembly moving?<P>The housing moving is a mounting problem. Check the 4 bolts and rubber mounting bushings.<P>Just the rack could mean you need a new rack bushing. The bushing is on the passenger side though... On mine it moved mostly on that side. I don't think there is a bushing on the drivers side. I think it's held in place by the pre-load adjustment for the pinion gear. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Coop88||07-16-2001||05:41 PM||||So if I replace the passenger side bushing, will that keep the bar in the rack from moving on both sides? Or will I have to disassemble this thing and look for something loose on the driver's side? Thanks for the input.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||07-16-2001||05:57 PM||||The passenger side bushing being bad will let the driver's side move some... But it really sounds like there is something wrong on the drivers side if it's moving <B>more</B> on that side. <P>If I remember right, the rack is sandwiched between the pinion gear and a spring loaded "shoe" (can't remember what it's called) that hold it in place. It sounds like the pinion bearing could be bad or the preload is out of adjustment. <P>Sounds like you need to disassemble and check it out unless someone else on here knows something else???<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Coop88||07-16-2001||08:02 PM||||After some tinkering and a trip to buy a 22mm wrench, I tightened the adjusting nut for the preload all the way down. When I tightened it all the way down, the movement on the rack evened out. By that I mean the driver's side moved just as much as the passenger side. But when I back the nut off 70-90*, like the service manual says, the driver's side moves more than the passenger side. So maybe the spring is worn inside? Could a worn pinion bearing cause this? Looks like dissasembly time.||||reg||5
Z||000006||Toddster||07-16-2001||08:37 PM||||If you only have ONE side moving something is broken. The inerds of the 88 rack consist of a solid steel rod that has teeth on the edge and threads on each end. The tie rods screw in on the ends and then are "crimped" into place (under the boots). If one side is moving and the other side does not then the threads may be stripped, the crimp is broken and the tie rod has unscrewed itself, or something snapped.<P>I've seen the aluminum housing broken on some racks. Check for cracks.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||07-16-2001||11:07 PM||||I assumed you meant up and down and back and forth movement... If it's moving in and out unequally you got something seriously wrong like Todd mentioned.<P>If it's moving in and out (equally) after adjusting the pre-load you have something wrong in the pinion area.<P>Back to up/down and back/front... Just looked in the manual to refresh my memory. The driver's end of the rack is held in place by the pinion gear on one side and the rack bearing on the other. I'd say a bad pinion gear would be the most likely suspect right now. A cracked housing, like Todd mentioned could do it. I guess a broken spring could do it too.<P>Sounds like exploratory surgery time. Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Coop88||07-23-2001||04:52 AM||||The rack is out. Later today I'll start taking it apart. Only hassle was I broke 3 of the 4 mounting bolts. I'm sure it will be fun removing the studs. Oh well.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bob Englert||07-23-2001||12:26 PM||||Use a cut off wheel in a dremel tool and cut the nut about 1/2 off (as far as you can with the dremel).  Then use a chisel and 1 or 2 hits will knock the nut off cleanly.  Just use regular high strength nuts and bolts when you reassemble.<P>It is much easier to do this with the spare tire tub out of the car.  Just be sure to unfasten the right side front brake line from the tub before removing.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000010||Toddster||07-23-2001||09:55 PM||||two relatively low cost solutions are to get a used rack at the nearby Pick&Pull. I've picked-up an entire '88 front end for $200...complete. so I imagine the steering rack alone would run $35 or so.<P>The other alternative is to go to the local Pontiac dealership and get a new bushing for $14 bucks and rebuild; assuming that there is nothing broken.<P>The best option is to call the Fiero Store and order one of their re-built units. They come with a brass bushing, two grease fittings that are not on stock racks, and completely painted to reduce corrosion. You just swap your tie rods onto it and re-align the front end. Its $230 bucks (I think) but you'll never have trouble with it again.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfiero||07-23-2001||11:15 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Toddster:<BR><B><P>The other alternative is to go to the local Pontiac dealership and get a new bushing for $14 bucks and rebuild; assuming that there is nothing broken.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The rack bushings are no longer available through GM......they were discontinued last Sept. (unless there is yet another part #).<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><P><B>The best option is to call the Fiero Store and order one of their re-built units. They come with a brass bushing, two grease fittings that are not on stock racks, and completely painted to reduce corrosion. You just swap your tie rods onto it and re-align the front end. Its $230 bucks (I think) but you'll never have trouble with it again.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I had thought about going this route, but look at what you get for $230......a new rack bushing and a pretty paint job.  The grease fittings are neat, but hardly worth the extra $$$.  Rodney sells rack bushings for $19 and some spray paint is $3-$5 for a total investment of less than $25.......where does  the other $205 go?  <P>Here is what I am doing/have done to my rack:<P>-new inner tie rods.....$27 ea. ($54 total)<BR>-new outer tie rods.....$40 ea. ($80 total)<BR>-new rack bushing (orig. plastic!).....$20 <BR>-black paint.....$4<BR>-"aluminum" paint.....$4<BR>-grease for worm gear.....$3<P>Grand total: $165.  This is for a virtually brand new rack.  It would have been 3 times that amount if bought from the Fiero Store (they want $90 apiece for inner tie rods!!).   I have nothing against them.....I've bought a lot of stuff from them in the past, but $230 for a rack with no tie rods is a bit much.     <P>BTW Coop.......my offer still stands if you can't salvage that rack.  LMK.  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 07-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Coop88||07-24-2001||08:03 PM||||After I dissassembled the rack, I found nothing broken or worn or anything. Since I have nothing to compare it to, I'm just looking for broken parts, cracks, or something grossly deformed. I'll look again and see if I can find anything. <P>I was just thinking- now that I know what the inside of the rack looks like, I think the movement I saw when I wiggled the wheel was the rack lifting up off the pinion. When I tightened the preload nut all the way down, it reduced the movement I saw in the rack. Didn't eliminate it completely, but it reduced it. When I backed it up 1/4 turn like the service manual says, the movement came right back. Could the spring inside the rack be worn out? How could I check it? Just put in a new spring? There is no play in the pinion, either. Thanks for the help so far.<P>I took another look. I still can't find anything wrong, except some play in the passenger side bushing. I'm gonna go ahead and replace it, then tighten the preload nut as much as I can. Hope it works.<p>[This message has been edited by Coop88 (edited 07-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Coop88||08-01-2001||12:23 PM||||I FINALLY had a chance to work on my steering rack. I've got an aluminum bushing in, and I've tighteded the preload nut. When I turn the pinion, it feels notchy. Turns easy with a wrench, but it just feels notchy. Should I replace the bearings?||||reg||10
